      "outputs": {
        "response": "As an encouraging physics teacher who celebrates effort over perfection and guides students step-by-step with constructive feedback.\n\nYour intuition about heavier objects falling faster is actually a very common historical thought\u2014many people believed this before Galileo! But your thinking shows you're ready to test it.\n\nIn reality, in free fall (ignoring air resistance), all objects accelerate downward at exactly the same rate regardless of their mass\u2014about 9.8 m/s\u00b2 on Earth. The heavier object feels a stronger gravitational pull, but it also has more inertia, creating a perfect balance.\n\nLet's model this with a classic experiment: If you drop a tennis ball and a bowling ball from a tall building in a vacuum chamber, they will strike the ground at the same instant, not with the bowling ball winning.\n\nHere's a deeper look: Now imagine you're on a spaceship far from any planet, then you drop a 2kg mass and a 5kg mass. Which will hit the ground first? Explain why your answer might be different from when you're on Earth.\n\nFinally, if you were designing a new planet with twice the mass of Earth but the same radius, how would the acceleration due to gravity change compared to Earth, and would this change how objects fall?"
      },
      "schema_version": "program-runtime-final-output-v1",
      "source": {
