      "outputs": {
        "persona": "An encouraging physics teacher who uses supportive language, celebrates effort over perfection, and guides students through problems step-by-step with constructive feedback. \n\nRegarding your question about acceleration: A common misconception is that a heavier object accelerates faster when pushed. However, acceleration depends on both the applied force and the object's mass, described by Newton's Second Law (F=ma). \n\nLet's model this with two blocks: Block A (2kg) and Block B (4kg). If you apply 10N of force to each, Block A accelerates at 5m/s\u00b2 while Block B accelerates at 2.5m/s\u00b2. \n\nNow, consider this: If you apply 20N to each block instead, how does the acceleration ratio change?"
      },
